# Use the Campaign API

A **campaign** is an immutable node/edge graph that walks players through a journey: enrol them,
send outreach on a message rail, evaluate goals, and route on the outcome. This skill is about
*consuming* the API — authoring a graph, publishing it, driving its lifecycle, and reading the
funnel. It is written from the Portal campaigns extension, which is the reference client.

> For building the rail a send node delivers on, load `beam-message-rail-federation`.

## The three things that surprise people

1. **Validation failures are HTTP 200 with `ok: false`.** Only a malformed DTO or bad paging
   args are 4xx. Always check `ok` before trusting `version`.
2. **Funnel stage keys are `beam_*`** (`beam_sent`, `beam_opened`, …) and each value is an
   **object** `{sealed, pending, lagging}`, not a number. The generated SDK client has not caught
   up on either — see [SDK drift](#sdk-drift).
3. **Campaign graphs are CID-scoped.** Every route needs only a CID-scoped requester; none of
   them takes a realm in the path. Omitting `realm` on the list returns every realm's campaigns.

## Auth and prefixes

All routes live under **`/api/campaigns`**. CID/PID come from the request context, never the URL.

- Every action carries `[RequirePermission("Campaigns", Read|Write)]` →
  **Admin, Developer, Tester** (and SuperAdmin, which short-circuits everything). Below Tester is
  a 403.
- One extra rule: a **`Write` from a zone-scoped request requires Admin**, whatever the role
  matrix says. The Portal campaigns extension is a zone extension, so its writes take that path.
- Headers: `Authorization: Bearer <operator token>`, the requester's scope header
  (`{cid}.{pid|ZONE_*}`), `Content-Type: application/json`.
- Author identity on writes comes from the token's **`accountId`**, not its `PlayerId` — an
  operator token often has no PlayerId for the realm. `audit` is **response-only**; sending it is
  silently ignored, so a client cannot claim authorship.

JSON is camelCase and enums serialize as strings.

## The surface

| Route | Auth | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /api/campaigns/draft` | Write | Save a draft; also the cheap validation probe |
| `POST /api/campaigns/publish` | Write | Publish — in place, or as a new version |
| `GET /api/campaigns` | Read | List, filter, page |
| `GET /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}` | Read | Fetch the graph |
| `GET /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}/status` | Read | Current lifecycle phase |
| `GET /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}/funnel?live=` | Read | Per-node send funnel |
| `POST /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}/deactivate` | Write | Drain or hard-stop |
| `POST /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}/archive` | Write | Retire a drained campaign |
| `POST /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}/reactivate` | Write | Restart an inactive one |
| `DELETE /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}` | Write | Delete an **unpublished** draft |

### `POST /draft`

Body is a raw `CampaignGraphDto`. Returns `SaveDraftResponseDto { ok, errors[] }`.

Validation runs first and **nothing is written** when it fails, which makes this the natural
"check my graph" call — it returns the full error list without publishing. A draft creates **no
directory row**: it is invisible to the runtime and `/status` 404s for it.

The save is a **whole-document replace**. Never build a graph from a partial read and save it.

### `POST /publish`

```jsonc
{ "graph": { /* CampaignGraphDto */ }, "priorMode": "Drain" }   // or "Hard"
```

Returns `PublishResponseDto { ok, version, isNewVersion, errors[], changes[] }`.

Publish does three things a client must understand:

- **It validates the graph**, exactly like `/draft`.
- **It asks each send node's rail whether it is configured in this realm.** Only a *definite*
  "not configured" yields `RailNotConfigured`; an unreachable rail is UNKNOWN and lets the publish
  through. The message names every missing key at once.
- **It classifies the change.** A `Content` edit (a message/offer ref, cosmetic config) applies
  **in place** at the same version — `isNewVersion: false`. A `Structural` change (shape, timing,
  targeting) publishes a **new version** and atomically cuts the prior one over to `WindingDown`
  (`priorMode: "Drain"`) or `Halting` (`priorMode: "Hard"`).

`changes[]` tells you which it was: `{ kind: "Structural" | "Content", message, node, edge, field }`.

### `GET /api/campaigns` — list

All query params optional; filters AND together. Offset paging with a total, not a cursor.

| param | type | default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `campaignId` | string | — | exact |
| `name` | string | — | case-insensitive **prefix** match |
| `phase` | enum | — | **repeatable**: `?phase=Draft&phase=Active` |
| `createdBy` / `publishedBy` | string | — | matches the account id **or** the stored email |
| `tag` | string | — | |
| `realm` | string | — | campaigns whose `acceptedRealms` contains this PID |
| `createdAfter` / `createdBefore` | ISO-8601 | — | |
| `publishedAfter` / `publishedBefore` | ISO-8601 | — | |
| `sort` | enum | `CreatedAt` | `CreatedAt`, `PublishedAt`, `LastUpdatedAt`, `Name`, `CampaignId` |
| `descending` | bool | `true` | |
| `skip` | int | `0` | must be ≥ 0 |
| `limit` | int | `50` | 1–200 |

`limit > 200` and `skip < 0` are **rejected as 400**, not clamped — so a caller asking for more
than is served learns about it instead of quietly paging wrong.

```json
{ "records": [ { "campaignId": "cmp-7f3a91c2", "version": 2, "name": "Lapsed whales",
                 "phase": "Active",
                 "createdBy": { "accountId": "112233", "email": "ops@studio.com", "kind": "User" },
                 "createdAt": "2026-09-01T12:04:11.000Z",
                 "publishedBy": { "accountId": "112233", "email": "ops@studio.com", "kind": "User" },
                 "publishedAt": "2026-09-03T09:41:02.000Z" } ],
  "total": 37, "skip": 0, "limit": 50 }
```

`total` is the count matching the same filter **before** the window. There is **one row per
(campaign, version)**, drafts included — a version with no directory row reports `"Draft"`. To
resolve "the current version", take the max version for that `campaignId`.

### `GET /{id}/{version}` and `/status`

`/{id}/{version}` returns the full `CampaignGraphDto` with `audit` populated; **404** if there is
no stored graph. `/status` returns `{ campaignId, version, phase }`; **404 for an unpublished
draft** — that 404 is the documented way to tell Draft from published.

`/status` is what you poll after every lifecycle command, because each one is an async
desired-state write.

## The lifecycle

```
Draft ──publish──▶ Launching ──(system: backfill drained)──▶ Active
                       │                                       │
                       ├──deactivate Drain──▶ WindingDown ◀─────┤
                       └──deactivate Hard───▶ Halting  ◀────────┘
                                    │              │
                    (MaxDrainDuration or deactivate Hard)
                                    ▼              ▼
                              (system: drained) Inactive ──archive──▶ Archived
                                                    └──reactivate──▶ Launching
```

`Launching → Active`, `WindingDown → Inactive` and `Halting → Inactive` are **system**
transitions with no endpoint — poll `/status`.

Runtime gates worth knowing: a player can be **enrolled** only in `Launching`/`Active`; in-flight
players keep **progressing** through `WindingDown` but stop at `Halting`; `Inactive`/`Archived`
are terminal and dropped from the runtime at zero cost.

### The lifecycle commands

All take no body except `deactivate`, and all return **200 with an empty body, or 409**.

- `deactivate` — `{"mode": "Drain"}` stops new enrolment and lets in-flight players finish;
  `{"mode": "Hard"}` force-exits them. `WindingDown → Halting` is a legal escalation.
- `archive` — `Inactive → Archived` only. Drain it first.
- `reactivate` — `Inactive → Launching`, a fresh era.
- `DELETE` — refuses once the version has been published (409); a live version is retired through
  the lifecycle, not deleted.

**409 is the expected answer to an inapplicable command, not a failure.** The commands are
idempotent: re-issuing one that already took effect returns 200 (`WindingDown→WindingDown`,
`Halting→Halting`, `Archived→Archived`, `Launching→Launching`).

## Reading the funnel

```
GET /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}/funnel            # live=true (default)
GET /api/campaigns/{id}/{version}/funnel?live=false # sealed only, one query, pending == 0
```

```json
{ "campaignId": "cmp-7f3a91c2", "version": 1,
  "nodes": [ { "node": "send-1-1-1",
    "stages": {
      "beam_queued":    { "sealed": 120, "pending": 3, "lagging": false },
      "beam_sent":      { "sealed": 118, "pending": 2, "lagging": false },
      "beam_delivered": { "sealed": 112, "pending": 1, "lagging": false },
      "beam_opened":    { "sealed":  41, "pending": 0, "lagging": false },
      "beam_clicked":   { "sealed":  12, "pending": 0, "lagging": false },
      "beam_converted": { "sealed":   7, "pending": 0, "lagging": false },
      "beam_goal_purchase@DE_1234": { "sealed": 9, "pending": 0, "lagging": false },
      "beam_met_purchase@DE_1234":  { "sealed": 7, "pending": 0, "lagging": false } } } ] }
```

### The stage keys

| key | meaning |
|---|---|
| `beam_queued` | the campaign handed the send to the rail — its own counter, never from outside |
| `beam_sent` | the rail federation acked it |
| `beam_delivered` · `beam_opened` · `beam_clicked` | progressively deeper engagement |
| `beam_converted` | runtime-produced when a player met an objective |
| `beam_goal_{label}` | players who **fired** one of that goal's observing leaves |
| `beam_met_{label}` | players who actually **met** that objective |

**`beam_goal_*` and `beam_met_*` are not the same number.** They coincide only for a single-leaf
`EventOccurred` goal; for `AllOf`/`AnyOf` they diverge. Label the first "goal events observed",
never "conversions", and never merge them.

`beam_` is the platform's reserved namespace — a graph whose authored event names sit inside it is
refused at publish with `ReservedEventName`.

### Reading a stage value

Display count is **`sealed + pending`**. Write a tolerant reader, because the value used to be a
bare number and your client and the gateway deploy independently:

```ts
type StageValue =
  | bigint | string
  | { sealed?: bigint | string | null; pending?: bigint | string | null; lagging?: boolean | null }
  | null | undefined;

const asNumber = (v: bigint | string | null | undefined) =>
  v == null ? 0 : (Number.isFinite(Number(v)) ? Number(v) : 0);

const toCount = (v: StageValue) =>
  v == null ? 0 : typeof v === 'object' ? asNumber(v.sealed) + asNumber(v.pending) : asNumber(v);

const isLagging = (v: StageValue) => v != null && typeof v === 'object' && v.lagging === true;
```

**`lagging: true` means the number is a floor, not a total.** The pending slice was not measured
because the rollup watermark trailed too far behind. A `0` under `lagging` means *unknown*, not
*none* — never render it as a plain zero. It is set campaign-wide and applied to every stage,
including stages with no counter at all.

`live=false` reports `pending: 0` — that zero means *not asked for*, not *nothing outstanding*.
Use it on any view that is not watching a campaign in flight: the live branch's second query scans
the collection the event firehose is writing to.

### Eventual consistency

A count lands in `pending` first and migrates into `sealed`. The write buffer flushes every ~2 s,
the rollup seals every ~5 s behind a ~5 s safety margin, and the composed read is cached ~3 s per
`(cid, instance, live)` — so polling faster than about 3 s returns the identical object.

### The shape is decided by the graph

A node the runtime has never touched still reports its **full** stage list as zeros. So an
all-zero funnel is real data meaning "nothing sent yet" — it is **404** that means "no such
version". Never conflate them.

If you aggregate across versions, walk newest-first and stop at the first version with nodes; an
older version's counters beat no counters at all.

## The graph DTO

```
CampaignGraphDto { campaignId, version, config, nodes[], edges[], audit? }

config: { name, options[], acceptedRealms[], tags[], maxDrainDurationMs? }
        options ∈ "AllowSimultaneousLanes" | "AllowCidLevelSegments"

node:   { id, lane?, body, outcomes[], incomingDecorators[], outgoingDecorators[] }
        outcomes[] = { key, timing: "Immediate" | "Delayed" }

body:   { kind: "Entry"|"Segment"|"Conditional"|"Schedule"|"Send",
          entry? | segment? | conditional? | schedule? | send?, customProperties? }

  entry       { trigger: { kind: "AutoSegment"|"Event"|"Inaction", segment?, event?, windowMs? },
                requalify: { kind: "Never"|"Immediately"|"After", cooldownMs? } }
  segment     { segment, evaluationWindowMs? }
  conditional { expression: Condition, maxWaitMs? }
  schedule    { kind: "DelayFor"|"SendNow"|"AtDateTime", delayMs?, when? }
  send        { railFederationId, targetPid, message, offer?, goals[],
                maxWaitMs, deliveryMaxWaitMs? }

edge:   { id, from, outcome, to, decorators[] }
```

`maxWaitMs` is the **conversion** window, measured from the rail's Sent ack. `deliveryMaxWaitMs`
is the rail's **delivery** ceiling (default 900 000 ms = 15 min). They are different promises and
both must be positive. A goal's `timerMs` must be ≤ `maxWaitMs`; leave it null to inherit.

### Two rules that will bite you

**Every PID any node references must be in `config.acceptedRealms`** — including a per-leaf
`observePid` override — or you get `PidNotInAcceptedRealms`.

**Never write a default you did not mean.** Omit `propertiesMode: "All"`, `target: "Value"`, empty
`values`/`properties` arrays, and null `withinMs`/`observePid`. Writing them changes the serialized
bytes, the change classifier reads that as **Structural**, and you fork a new immutable campaign
version over nothing.

**Goal labels become Mongo field names.** No `.`, no leading `$` (`InvalidGoalLabel`); unique
within a node (`DuplicateGoalLabel`). Derive them from a stable id, never a display name — the
label keys `beam_met_{label}`, so renaming orphans the conversion history. The Portal convention is
`{goalId}@{pid}` per scope, bare `{goalId}` for a cross-scope `AllOf`.

## Validation errors

Returned inside a **200** body with `ok: false`, as
`{ code, message, node?, edge?, goalLabel? }`. Read the coordinates; never parse the message.

Graph shape: `DuplicateNodeId`, `DuplicateEdgeId`, `EdgeReferencesUnknownNode`,
`EdgeOutcomeNotDeclared`, `GraphHasCycle`, `NodeUnreachable`,
`LaneHasMultipleAutoSegmentEntries`.

Targeting: `SendTargetPidRequired`, `GoalObservePidRequired`, `PidNotInAcceptedRealms`,
`CidLevelSegmentNotAllowed`.

Goals: `GoalPredicateEmpty`, `GoalAbsenceWindowInvalid`, `GoalTimerExceedsMaxWait`,
`GoalPropertyBoundInvalid`, `DuplicateGoalLabel`, `InvalidGoalLabel`, `ReservedEventName`.

Windows: `SendDeliveryWindowInvalid`, `SendConversionWindowInvalid`.

Publish only: **`RailNotConfigured`** — the rail exists but reports itself unconfigured in this
realm.

HTTP-level: `400 InvalidCampaignGraph` (a DTO whose `kind` tag and populated branch disagree),
`400 InvalidCampaignPageLimit` / `InvalidCampaignPageSkip`, `401`, `403`, `404`, `409`.

## SDK drift

The generated web SDK client (`campaignsPostDraft`, `campaignsPostPublish`, `campaignsGet`,
`campaignsGetByCampaignIdAndVersion`, `campaignsDelete`, `campaignsGetStatus`,
`campaignsGetFunnel`, `campaignsPostDeactivate`, `campaignsPostArchive`, `campaignsPostReactivate`)
is generated from an OpenAPI document published on its own cadence, so it trails the gateway.
Known gaps to compensate for by hand:

| gap | consequence |
|---|---|
| `campaignsGetFunnel` has no `live` argument | every generated funnel read is the expensive live path; reach `live=false` through the raw requester |
| `CampaignNodeFunnelDto.stages` is typed `Record<string, bigint \| string>` | there is **no generated `CampaignStageFunnelDto`** — declare the union yourself |
| `PropertyBoundDto` lacks `setOp`, `values`, `target` | hand-augment the type |
| `GoalPredicateDto` lacks `observePid` | hand-augment the type |
| `sort` on `campaignsGet` is `unknown` | pass the string yourself |

General shape caveats: **every generated field is optional**, 64-bit fields arrive as
`bigint | string`, and date fields arrive as `Date`. **Never `Number()` a `bigint | string` id** —
ids exceed the safe integer range. Counters are small populations, so a checked conversion is fine
for them.

Reference client layout (Portal campaigns extension): a single transport module owning every SDK
call, a domain layer over it, and one `wire.ts` boundary that re-exports the generated types and
holds the hand-written augmentations, each marked "delete once the generated DTO has it".

## End to end

```http
GET  /api/campaigns?realm=DE_1234&phase=Active&sort=CreatedAt&descending=true&limit=50
POST /api/campaigns/draft      { …CampaignGraphDto… }        → 200 { "ok": true, "errors": [] }
POST /api/campaigns/publish    { "graph": {…}, "priorMode": "Drain" }
                                                             → 200 { "ok": true, "version": 1,
                                                                     "isNewVersion": true }
GET  /api/campaigns/cmp-7f3a91c2/1/status                    → poll "Launching" → "Active"
GET  /api/campaigns/cmp-7f3a91c2/1/funnel                    → per-node stages
POST /api/campaigns/cmp-7f3a91c2/1/deactivate { "mode": "Drain" }
GET  /api/campaigns/cmp-7f3a91c2/1/status                    → poll until "Inactive"
POST /api/campaigns/cmp-7f3a91c2/1/archive
```

## Checklist

- [ ] Check `ok` on every draft/publish response before trusting `version`
- [ ] Read funnel stage values as objects, summing `sealed + pending`, and honour `lagging`
- [ ] Never render a `lagging` zero as a confident zero
- [ ] Treat 404 as "no such version" and an all-zero funnel as "nothing sent yet"
- [ ] Treat 409 from a lifecycle command as the expected answer, not an error
- [ ] Pass `realm` on the list unless you really want every realm in the CID
- [ ] Omit defaults you did not mean, or you will fork a campaign version over nothing
- [ ] Put every referenced PID in `config.acceptedRealms`
- [ ] Derive goal labels from stable ids, with no `.` and no leading `$`
- [ ] Poll `/status` after each lifecycle command rather than assuming it applied
- [ ] Use `live=false` on any view not watching a campaign in flight
